#!/usr/bin/env bash
# uninstall: remove what `init` installed. (optional)
#
# Run by dot-uninstall, only when it is given --remove-plugins, for every enabled plugin whose
# `installed` hook succeeds, last enabled first. It runs before the dotfiles (including this
# plugin's folder) are removed, after dot-uninstall has saved a "pre-uninstall" backup that
# includes the data from your `backup` hook. Afterwards `installed` runs again; if it still
# succeeds the plugin is reported as failed, so make `installed` notice what you removed.
# Without this file the plugin's software is left in place.
#
# Contract
#   Exit 0      success, or nothing to do.
#   Exit != 0   failure: reported, and dot-uninstall carries on. The software stays; remove it
#               by hand.
#   Remove what `init` put on the machine (SDK folders, packages you added for this plugin);
#   leave the user's own data (projects, images, volumes) alone, and say so in a comment.
#   Be careful with deletes: use `rm -rf "${DIR:?}"` so an empty variable can't expand to /.
#   Never remove system packages other software may need.
#   Same rules as `init`: non-interactive (stdin closed), no time limit, and a `needs-sudo`
#   file in the plugin folder gets the sudo password asked for before this hook starts.
#   Think twice before shipping one at all: removing a graphics driver, say, can leave a
#   machine without a display, and "no uninstall hook" is a fine answer.
#
# Environment
#   DOT_PLUGIN, DOT_PLUGIN_DIR, DOT_OS, DOT_ROOT, DOT_COMMON: as in `init`.
#   DOT_HOOK        "uninstall"
#
# Arguments: none.
